---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import FlowDiagram from '../components/FlowDiagram.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
const source = 'https://github.com/paddo-tech/tether-cli/blob/main/';
---

<BaseLayout title="Security — Tether CLI" description="See how Tether encrypts dotfiles, checks synced packages before it installs them, and decides which machines to trust.">
  <main id="main">
    <section class="page-hero wrap"><div><p class="eyebrow"><Icon name="lock" size={15} /> SECURITY, IN THE OPEN</p><h1>Your files.<br /><em>Your keys.</em></h1><p class="lede">Tether encrypts dotfiles on your machine before they reach Git. It checks synced packages before it installs them. You control the repository, the passphrase and the machines you trust.</p><a class="text-link" href={`${source}src/security/encryption.rs`}>Read the encryption code <span>↗</span></a></div><div class="security-summary"><span class="summary-icon"><Icon name="lock" size={36} /></span><h2>Encrypted before<br />they leave.</h2><dl><div><dt>Personal dotfiles</dt><dd>AES-256-GCM</dd></div><div><dt>Encryption key</dt><dd>Protected with age</dd></div><div><dt>Storage</dt><dd>Your Git repository</dd></div><div><dt>Synced packages</dt><dd>Checked before install</dd></div><div><dt>Machine records</dt><dd>Signed with SSH keys</dd></div></dl></div></section>
    <section class="content-section wrap" id="encryption"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">FOLLOW A FILE</p><h2>Plaintext here.<br />Encrypted in Git.</h2><p>Your local shell reads your dotfiles normally. Tether stores an encrypted copy in your sync repository.</p></div><FlowDiagram label="PERSONAL DOTFILE ENCRYPTION" caption="Encryption is enabled by default. Package manifests remain readable in Git." steps={[
      { icon: 'file', title: 'Local dotfile', detail: '.zshrc · Readable on your machine' },
      { icon: 'lock', title: 'Encrypt locally', detail: 'AES-256-GCM · Fresh nonce' },
      { icon: 'git', title: 'Sync through Git', detail: '.zshrc.enc · Encrypted contents' },
    ]} /><div class="detail-columns"><article><h3>Authenticated encryption.</h3><p>Tether uses a 256-bit key and a new random 96-bit nonce for each encryption operation.</p><p>The authentication tag detects changes to the encrypted contents.</p></article><article><h3>A portable encrypted key.</h3><p>Tether protects your encryption key with age and your passphrase. The encrypted key travels with your Git repo.</p><p>Your passphrase does not go into the repository.</p></article><article><h3>Local files stay readable.</h3><p>Your shell needs plaintext dotfiles. Protect your devices and local accounts, including the cached encryption key.</p><p>Encryption in Git does not protect an unlocked machine.</p></article></div></section>
    <section class="split-section wrap" id="key-management"><div class="section-copy"><p class="eyebrow">ONE PASSPHRASE. YOUR MACHINES.</p><h2>Unlock your setup.<br /><em>Keep control.</em></h2><p>The first machine creates an encryption key. Other machines retrieve the encrypted key from your repo.</p><p>Run <code>tether unlock</code> to decrypt and cache it locally. Run <code>tether lock</code> to clear the cache.</p><p>Locking clears the key cache. Your local plaintext dotfiles remain on disk.</p><Code code={'tether unlock\ntether lock'} /></div><FlowDiagram label="ON YOUR NEXT MACHINE" caption="The decrypted key cache stays local. Tether does not sync it to Git." steps={[
      { icon: 'git', title: 'Get the key', detail: 'encryption.key.age from your repo' },
      { icon: 'key', title: 'Enter your passphrase', detail: 'Decrypt the key with age' },
      { icon: 'file', title: 'Read your dotfiles', detail: 'Decrypt and apply locally' },
    ]} /></section>
    <section class="content-section wrap" id="supply-chain"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow"><Icon name="shield" size={15} /> CHECK BEFORE YOU INSTALL</p><h2>In the manifest.<br />Not yet approved.</h2><p>Anyone who can push to your repo can add a line to a manifest. Tether checks each synced package before a package manager runs.</p></div><FlowDiagram label="FROM MANIFEST TO INSTALL" caption="A package that fails a check waits in the approval inbox on this machine." steps={[
      { icon: 'git', title: 'Manifest change', detail: 'A package arrives from another machine' },
      { icon: 'shield', title: 'Run the checks', detail: 'Trusted record · Name · Tap · Release age · OSV' },
      { icon: 'check', title: 'Install or hold', detail: 'Install it, or hold it in the inbox' },
    ]} /><div class="detail-columns"><article><h3>A release-age limit.</h3><p>npm, pnpm, bun and uv skip releases newer than 7 days. Set <code>packages.min_release_age_days</code> to change the limit. 0 turns it off.</p><p>Some managers cannot enforce the limit: gem, npm before 11.10, pnpm before 10.16 and bun before 1.3. Their synced packages wait for approval.</p></article><article><h3>Install scripts off.</h3><p>npm, pnpm and bun install and upgrade with install scripts turned off.</p><p>Add a package to <code>packages.allow_scripts</code> when it needs its scripts. Older npm and pnpm cannot limit scripts to one package, so its scripts stay off there.</p></article><article><h3>Malware checks with OSV.</h3><p>Tether checks npm, pnpm, bun, uv and gem packages against OSV before it installs or upgrades them. A <code>MAL-</code> advisory blocks that release.</p><p>Other advisories show a warning. When OSV cannot be reached, a sync still installs. The dashboard and <code>tether packages approve</code> ask first.</p></article></div><div class="detail-columns"><article><h3>Names, not commands.</h3><p>Tether skips manifest names that look like flags, URLs, paths, tarballs or <code>git+</code> specs.</p><p>Every package manager runs in an empty directory, <code>~/.tether/run</code>. A local file or project config cannot change what it installs.</p></article><article><h3>Taps you chose.</h3><p>Homebrew taps outside <code>homebrew/*</code> must be in <code>packages.brew.trusted_taps</code>. Other taps, and their formulae and casks, wait in the inbox.</p><p>Tether finds the tap of a short name, such as <code>bun</code>, before it installs it. Upgrades name only packages from trusted taps.</p></article><article><h3>Exact versions.</h3><p>Manifests record the version each machine installed, such as <code>typescript@5.6.3</code>. The Brewfile stays unpinned.</p><p>A sync installs the newest version that a trusted machine lists. A manifest cannot pick an older version.</p></article></div></section>
    <section class="split-section wrap" id="machine-trust"><div class="section-copy"><p class="eyebrow">TRUST A MACHINE ONCE</p><h2>Signed records.<br /><em>Your trust list.</em></h2><p>Each machine has its own SSH signing key. Every sync signs the machine record that lists its packages and versions.</p><p>Your trust list stays on this machine and never syncs. A package installs on its own only when a trusted, signed record lists that exact version.</p><p>Trust is transitive. When you trust a machine, you also trust the packages it installed. A package you rejected on this machine stays blocked.</p><Code code={'tether machines list\ntether machines trust <id> <fingerprint>'} /></div><FlowDiagram label="ON EACH SYNC" caption="Tether never moves trust to another machine id on its own." steps={[
      { icon: 'key', title: 'Sign the record', detail: 'machines/<id>.json · ed25519 key' },
      { icon: 'shield', title: 'Verify locally', detail: 'Match the key in ~/.tether/trusted_keys' },
      { icon: 'box', title: 'Install listed packages', detail: 'Hold all other packages in the inbox' },
    ]} /></section>
    <section class="content-section wrap" id="approval-inbox"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">YOU DECIDE</p><h2>One inbox.<br />Every held item.</h2><p>Held packages, taps and machine keys wait in <code>~/.tether/inbox.json</code>. The inbox never syncs. Review it in the terminal or on the dashboard Security tab.</p></div><div class="security-grid"><article class="surface-panel"><Icon name="shield" size={27} /><h3>What the inbox holds.</h3><ul class="check-list"><li>Packages that no trusted machine record lists</li><li>Packages from untrusted Homebrew taps</li><li>Packages whose manager cannot enforce the release-age limit</li><li>Releases with a <code>MAL-</code> advisory, which you cannot approve</li><li>A release for this OS, when the version that another OS pins fails here</li><li>New machine keys and changed machine keys</li></ul><Code code={'tether packages inbox\ntether packages approve <id>\ntether packages reject <id>'} /></article><article class="surface-panel"><Icon name="lock" size={27} /><h3>What the checks cannot promise.</h3><p>Homebrew has no release-age limit and no OSV data. Tether does not check brew packages against OSV.</p><p>OSV lists known malware only. A new malicious release can pass until someone reports it.</p><p>Trust follows the machine. When you trust a machine, you trust the packages its user installs on it.</p><p>Commit signatures are an audit trail only. Record signatures decide trust.</p><a class="text-link" href="/docs#package-security">Read the package security guide <span>↗</span></a></article></div><div class="detail-columns"><article><h3>Old records do not count.</h3><p>Each record has a generation that increases with every save. Tether keeps the newest generation it accepted from each key.</p><p>An older signed record, such as one from git history, grants no trust.</p></article><article><h3>Changed keys stop.</h3><p>When a trusted machine signs with a different key, Tether warns you. It does not trust that record until you approve the new key.</p><p>Tether ignores a record that fails its signature, and shows a warning.</p></article><article><h3>Your record, from your copy.</h3><p>This machine builds its record from <code>~/.tether/machine.json</code>, not from the repo.</p><p>A change that someone pushes to the repo copy does not get your signature.</p></article></div></section>
    <section class="content-section wrap" id="secret-detection"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">CHECK BEFORE YOU SYNC</p><h2>Spot credentials<br />in your configs.</h2><p>Tether scans files for potential secrets. Review the warnings before you continue syncing.</p></div><div class="security-grid"><article class="surface-panel"><Icon name="file" size={27} /><h3>What the scanner looks for.</h3><ul class="check-list"><li>AWS access keys and GitHub tokens</li><li>API keys and bearer tokens</li><li>SSH and RSA private keys</li><li>Passwords and database credentials</li><li>High-entropy strings</li></ul></article><article class="surface-panel"><Icon name="lock" size={27} /><h3>What the scanner cannot promise.</h3><p>Pattern matching can miss secrets or flag harmless text. Review your configuration and choose what you sync.</p><p>Use a private repository. Package manifests and repository metadata remain visible to people with repository access.</p><a class="text-link" href={`${source}src/security/secrets.rs`}>Review scanning patterns <span>↗</span></a></article></div></section>
    <section class="content-section wrap" id="threat-model"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">KNOW THE BOUNDARIES</p><h2>Understand the protection.</h2></div><div class="detail-columns"><article><h3>Encryption protects stored contents.</h3><p>Repository access alone does not reveal encrypted dotfile contents without the key or passphrase.</p><p>AES-GCM detects tampering with encrypted data. Git transport security depends on your SSH or HTTPS configuration.</p></article><article><h3>Your repo can change, too.</h3><p>Anyone with push access can edit manifests and machine records. Signed records and your local trust list limit what a sync installs from those changes.</p><p>Protect each machine’s <code>~/.tether/signing_key</code>. Anyone with that key can sign records as that machine.</p></article><article><h3>Your device needs protection, too.</h3><p>Malware and compromised local accounts can access plaintext files or cached keys.</p><p>Use device encryption, a strong passphrase, and two-factor authentication for your Git host. Review secret warnings before syncing.</p></article></div></section>
    <section class="source-section wrap"><div><p class="eyebrow">VERIFY THE DETAILS</p><h2>The code is open.</h2><p>Read the implementation and its tests.</p></div><div class="source-links"><a href={`${source}src/security/encryption.rs`}><span>File encryption<small>encryption.rs</small></span><span>↗</span></a><a href={`${source}src/security/keychain.rs`}><span>Key management<small>keychain.rs</small></span><span>↗</span></a><a href={`${source}src/security/secrets.rs`}><span>Secret scanning<small>secrets.rs</small></span><span>↗</span></a><a href={`${source}src/packages/policy.rs`}><span>Package policy<small>policy.rs</small></span><span>↗</span></a><a href={`${source}src/packages/osv.rs`}><span>OSV checks<small>osv.rs</small></span><span>↗</span></a><a href={`${source}src/sync/signing.rs`}><span>Record signing and trust<small>signing.rs</small></span><span>↗</span></a><a href={`${source}src/packages/inbox.rs`}><span>Approval inbox<small>inbox.rs</small></span><span>↗</span></a><a href={`${source}Cargo.toml`}><span>Dependencies<small>Cargo.toml</small></span><span>↗</span></a></div></section>
    <section class="report-section wrap"><Icon name="lock" size={25} /><div><h2>Report a security issue.</h2><p>Email <a href="mailto:security@tether-cli.com">security@tether-cli.com</a> with the affected version, reproduction steps, and potential impact.</p><p>Keep vulnerability details out of public issues until a fix is available.</p></div></section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
